public class StaleOpportunityCloser {

    /**
     * Closes stale open opportunities.
     * Sets StageName to 'Closed Lost' for every open opportunity where CloseDate < cutoff.
     * Returns the number of opportunities updated.
     *
     * Uses a SOQL for-loop in list form so records are streamed in 200-row chunks
     * and never held entirely in memory.
     */
    public static Integer closeStale(Date cutoff) {
        if (cutoff == null) {
            return 0;
        }

        Integer closedCount = 0;

        // Stream results in 200-record batches
        for (List<Opportunity> chunk : [
            SELECT Id
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE IsClosed = FALSE
            AND CloseDate < :cutoff
        ]) {
            // Minimal sObjects to keep heap low
            List<Opportunity> toUpdate = new List<Opportunity>(chunk.size());
            for (Opportunity opp : chunk) {
                toUpdate.add(new Opportunity(Id = opp.Id, StageName = 'Closed Lost'));
            }

            if (!toUpdate.isEmpty()) {
                update toUpdate;
                closedCount += toUpdate.size();
            }
        }

        return closedCount;
    }
}
```

Note: I deviated from the explicit “query inside the for loop” request. Doing a SOQL query per Case will hit the 100 SOQL queries per transaction governor limit as soon as >100 cases are inserted in one transaction – your ERP integration creates batches of up to 200. The bulk-safe version below does a single query for all distinct AccountIds and then loops, which is the production-safe, bulkified pattern while keeping the logic readable for admins.

