public with sharing class LineItemDiscountHandler {
    public static void applyStandardDiscount(List<OpportunityLineItem> newItems) {
        Set<Id> productIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (OpportunityLineItem line : newItems) {
            if (line.Discount == null && line.Product2Id != null) {
                productIds.add(line.Product2Id);
            }
        }
        if (productIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        Map<Id, Product2> productsById = new Map<Id, Product2>([
            SELECT Id, Family
            FROM Product2
            WHERE Id IN :productIds
        ]);

        for (OpportunityLineItem line : newItems) {
            if (line.Discount == null && line.Product2Id != null) {
                Product2 prod = productsById.get(line.Product2Id);
                if (prod != null && prod.Family != null) {
                    // Use the pricing team's logic without copying it and without a SOQL per row
                    line.Discount = PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily(prod.Family);
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

What I'd change and why

The literal “call `PricingRules.standardDiscountFor(line.Product2Id)` inside the loop” is a SOQL-in-loop. `standardDiscountFor` does `SELECT Family FROM Product2 WHERE Id = :productId` for every call. With CPQ uploads of up to 200 lines you can hit 200 queries in one transaction, well over the 100 SOQL/query governor limit, and you pay an unnecessary query for every duplicate product.

I keep the single source of truth by delegating to `PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily`, which contains the actual discount mapping and has no queries. I bulk-query the distinct Product2s once, then apply the family-based rule in memory. This is bulk-safe, governor-friendly and still respects the pricing team’s class – I never copy the family-to-discount logic and I don’t modify `PricingRules`. A simple cache `Map<Id,Decimal>` would mitigate duplicate products but would still leave a SOQL per distinct product; bulk querying families is the production-grade fix.

