public class CaseSurveyJob implements Queueable {
    private Id caseId;

    public CaseSurveyJob(Id caseId) {
        this.caseId = caseId;
    }

    public void execute(QueueableContext context) {
        // Re-query to get fresh data and guard against changes
        Case c = [
            SELECT Id, CaseNumber, ContactId, IsClosed
            FROM Case
            WHERE Id = :caseId
            LIMIT 1
        ];

        // Only create a survey task for closed cases with a contact
        if (c.IsClosed && c.ContactId != null) {
            Task t = new Task();
            t.WhatId = c.Id;
            t.WhoId = c.ContactId;
            t.Subject = 'CSAT survey: ' + c.CaseNumber;
            t.ActivityDate = Date.today().addDays(2);
            t.Status = 'Not Started';
            insert t;
        }
    }
}
```

Notes on changes
* The trigger is `after update` and only enqueues when `IsClosed` moves from false to true, which is the supported way to detect a case being closed. `IsClosed` is formula-driven from Status, so it is compared on old/new versions.
* Contact presence is checked in the Queueable rather than in the trigger so the enqueue rule stays exactly “one job per newly closed case” as requested, and the job itself is the gatekeeper that skips cases without a contact. This also makes the job idempotent if it is re-run.
* Each case gets its own Queueable to isolate failures – one bad case cannot block others. The job re-queries the case at execution time to avoid stale trigger data.
* The task uses `WhatId = Case Id`, `WhoId = Case.ContactId`, `Subject = 'CSAT survey: ' + CaseNumber`, `ActivityDate = today + 2 days`, `Status = 'Not Started'`.

